  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Mikhail Jekovich panics when he discovers the pony has escaped from the locker room and is roaming the upper floors. Meanwhile, Timur opens the polling booth with red and green ballots, and Nikita's father spots Denis still in Moscow.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Где это глупое животное сейчас?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Как этого коня хотя бы зовут?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Ищите его быстрее наверх!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "timur",
      "t": "У нас два вида бюллетеней.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "timur",
      "t": "Взял бюллетень и пошёл проголосовал.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "timur",
      "t": "Я в кабинке независимый наблюдатель.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Здравствуйте, мой рейс сегодня перенесли.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я поэтому ещё не в Японии.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "До деловой встречи есть время.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Так что мы можем пообедать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Не могу, я на диете.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Давай выпьем по бокалу коньяка.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Где это глупое животное сейчас?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Where is that foolish animal right now?' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'where' and finishing on 'now'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Как этого коня хотя бы зовут?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What is this horse's name at least?' Mikhail anchors the line on 'how', while 'is called' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Ищите его быстрее наверх!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Look for him upstairs quickly!' The grammar packs 'to look for' and 'upstairs' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "timur",
      "t": "У нас два вида бюллетеней.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We have two kinds of ballots.' Notice how Russian expresses 'at' and 'ballots' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "timur",
      "t": "Взял бюллетень и пошёл проголосовал.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Take a ballot and go vote.' Timur anchors the line on 'took', while 'voted' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "timur",
      "t": "Я в кабинке независимый наблюдатель.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'In the booth I am an independent observer.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'I' and finishing on 'observer'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Здравствуйте, мой рейс сегодня перенесли.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Hello, they postponed my flight today.' The plural past ending '-li' on the verb for 'to carry over' covers a group or the polite 'you'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я поэтому ещё не в Японии.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'That is why I am not in Japan yet.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'I' before landing on 'Japan'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "До деловой встречи есть время.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'There is time before the business meeting.' Mikhail brings out the contrast between 'until' and 'business' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Так что мы можем пообедать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'So we can have lunch.' Mikhail anchors the line on 'so', while 'to have lunch' completes the thought. Denis answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Не могу, я на диете.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I cannot, I am on a diet.' Denis anchors the line on 'not', while 'diet' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Давай выпьем по бокалу коньяка.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Let us drink a glass of cognac.' The meaning hinges on 'come on' working together with 'cognac' in spoken Russian. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
